Pasi Kallinen wrote:
> the goodpos() function (from teleport.c) is used to see if the location
> is valid. If not, then we choose another location close to it.
> If no good place is found, then the player could end up in
> a stone wall.

The game should fall back on the current trapdoor behavior if no good
place can be found, although it should probably try all spaces within
two of the destination before sending the player to a random location.
It may not be logical, but it'd be fair - and who knows what kind of
diverting, cross-connecting tunnels might there be between dungeon
levels, intersecting with hole and trapdoor shafts?

However...

Okay, a trapdoor's destination is determined the first time the player
falls down it, right?  So that's going to be a known goodpos at that
time.  If the player does something that involves water appearing on
that spot (and there really aren't a lot of ways to do that), then I'd
say that the player *should* be plopped down into that pool, with all
the penalties and death potential that that entails.  After all, the
player knows where that hole leads, it was safe once, and all the ways
of making pools come as a result of the player's actions.

